.TH DISPLAY.D 4 "31 October 2026" "amix-base"
.SH NAME
display.d \- the display cards X can use, one file each
.SH SYNOPSIS
.BI /etc/display.d/ card
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each display card's package puts one file in
.BR /etc/display.d ,
named for the card, that tells
.BR xstart (1)
how to find the card and run its X server.
The file is the card's own data, replaced with its package; it is not
edited.
Which card is used, and in which mode, is in
.BR /etc/display ,
not here (see
.BR display (4)).
.PP
Each line is a key and its value, separated by blanks or tabs; a
.B #
starts a comment.
.TP
.BI node " path"
the card's device node, opened to see whether the card is there.
.TP
.BI module " name"
the kernel module that drives it, when there is one.
.TP
.BI server " path"
the X server.
.TP
.BI modes " W" x "H ..."
the screen sizes the card and its server take, the default first; or
.B auto
when the server picks the mode itself.
The card's mode list is kept here and nowhere else.
.TP
.BI depths " bits ..."
the pixel depths, the default first.
.TP
.BI args " arguments"
the server's arguments after the display number:
.B %m
is the mode,
.B %w
and
.B %h
its width and height,
.B %d
the depth and
.B %n
the display number.
.TP
.BI env " name" = "value ..."
the server's environment, with the same
.BR % -names;
it reaches the server alone, never the session's programs.
.TP
.BI limits " name" = "KB ..."
the limits raised before the server starts:
.B vmem
(the address space, for a card whose memory the server maps whole) and
.B data
(the server's own memory).
.TP
.B monsw yes \fR|\fP no
whether the card shares the monitor with the chipset display, so that the
monitor switch of
.BR display (4)
applies.
When the kernel's
.B monsw
module answers, it switches the monitor itself; otherwise the tool below
does, before the server starts and back when the session ends.
.TP
.BI monswtool " path"
that tool, given the switch value and
.B rtg
or
.BR off .
.TP
.BI restore " command"
run, by its full path, after a session whose server ended on its own
(it crashed or was killed) rather than being stopped at the session's end;
for a card whose server must give the keyboard back to the console itself.
.TP
.BI console " command"
reserved for the card's console takeover; no tool reads it yet.
.TP
.BI name " text"
the card's name in words.
.SH CARDS
.TP
.B native
the chipset display with the system's own X11R4 server, shipped by the base
package.
The server is monochrome and picks its own mode: 1024x768 on an A2024 or a
Moniterm, 640x480 on any other monitor.
.B SCREEN_GROUP
is the Alt-function-key screen group the server takes; it is the display
number, so
.B "xstart :10"
puts X on Alt-F10, as the system's own files do.
This card is a best effort: the server and
.B xinit
start, but on an emulated machine no session was seen to reach the server;
it may wait until its screen group is shown (Alt-F10).
.PP
The other cards' files come with their drivers' packages.
The system's A2410 and DMI Resolver servers have no file: no one has been
able to try them; their arguments are in the comments of the stock
.B Xservers
file of
.BR xdm .
.SH EXAMPLE
The Z3660's file,
.BR /etc/display.d/z3660 ,
as its driver package ships it:
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
# /etc/display.d/z3660 -- the Z3660 RTG card (display.d(4))
name      Z3660 RTG
node      /dev/zz9000
module    z3660rtg
server    /opt/amix/X11R6/bin/Xzz9000
modes     1024x768 640x480 800x600 1280x720 1280x800 1280x1024 1920x1080
depths    16
args      -mode %m -ac -kb
limits    vmem=131072 data=65536
monsw     yes
monswtool /opt/amix/bin/zz9k_monsw
restore   /opt/amix/bin/rtg-kbdback
.ft R
.fi
.PP
The first mode, 1024x768, is the default.
The server finds its fonts and its colour database by itself (the X11R6.3
package from 6.3-2 on), and links its X libraries in, so the file gives no
.BR \-fp ,
no
.B \-co
and no
.BR env .
The limits cover the card's memory, which the server maps whole, and the
server's own memory.
The monitor switch's value is in
.BR /etc/display ,
not here.
.PP
The chipset's file,
.BR /etc/display.d/native :
.PP
.nf
.ft CW
node     /dev/console
server   /usr/X/bin/X
modes    auto
depths   1
env      SCREEN_GROUP=%n
monsw    no
.ft R
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR display (4),
.BR xstart (1)
